("lore", "Elves", "Elfshots and night pressure",
 ["Some elves arrive as sickness, and the old books name them plainly. Old English leechbooks, close cousins in tongue, speak of ælfe sending stitch and fever, elf-shot, and prescribe herbs and charms against them. Northern pages keep the same pair: the Sigrdrífumál warns of harmful runes to avoid, sagas name álfar touching bodies from outside, and later folk tell of changelings and night-pressers, mara, riding sleepers. Touch from outside, named as elf.",
  "Separate watching from curing and both stay honest. That elves and mara were blamed for illness is Attested in charms and saga pattern. That any charm cured anyone is not shown, and this book prescribes nothing. Folk gathering of protective herbs and swept thresholds belongs to custom, related, not cure. Your use here is Modern and small: dated lines, clean room, watched sleep, doctor kept for doctor's work.",
  "Try the honest part for one week at home. Air the bedroom fully, shake bedding, sweep under the bed, note date aired and weather. Keep sleep hours steady and log dreams beside your Worlds pages. If illness comes, see a clinician and write that visit down too. Clean, dated, referred.",
  "<b>Lore Root:</b> ælfe-shot in leechbooks plus álfar and mara as harm — Attested pattern; no cure claimed here."],
 "How sleeps your room this week, and what one cleaning would ease it?"),
("lore", "Dwarfs", "Svartálfaheimr: the dark-elf home",
 ["One lane holds two names for the same smithy-door. The poems name Svartálfaheimr, home of black-elves, as the place Loki visits to commission treasures, while Snorri elsewhere calls such smiths dwarfs dwelling in earth and rock. Whether dark-elves and dwarfs were ever fully separate, no old page says plainly. Ships and hammers come out of that door either way. One workshop, two signs.",
  "Keep the blur labeled and it stops troubling you. That Svartálfaheimr stands as smith-home is Attested in Eddic poems. That Snorri sorts light-elves above and dark-elves or dwarfs below is Attested as his late system, not as Viking street-map. This book knocks on the smithy-door under both names and takes the work as the teaching: skill underground, price above.",
  "Name your own underground this week, the shed, the cellar shelf, the toolbox under the sink. Clear it fully in half an hour, cloth and bag at elbow, logging what you found and what you mended. Work below, order above. Date the clearing beside your dwarf-names.",
  "<b>Lore Root:</b> Svartálfaheimr smith-home in poems plus Snorri sorting — Attested, late system."],
 "What is your underground, shed or shelf or toolbox, and when will you clear it?"),
